\title{Batch Smoothness Testing: Exactness, Cost Dichotomy,\\ and the Amdahl Ceiling}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{29 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Product-tree batch smoothness testing replaces per-candidate trial division by a
single remainder-tree computation shared across a pool of candidates. We prove an
exactness criterion: for $0 < n < 2^t$, the integer $n$ is $B$-smooth if and only
if $n$ divides $P_B^{\,t}$, where $P_B$ is the product of the primes at most $B$.
The criterion is sharp, since $2^t \mid P_B^{\,s}$ holds exactly when $t \le s$,
and it survives both implementation shortcuts actually used --- modular reduction
inside the remainder tree and repeated squaring --- as well as being independent
of the shape of the product tree. Consequently an empirical exact-match audit
(agreement of the batch-detected and trial-detected smooth sets on $500$ of $500$
samples, in three implementation variants) is upgraded to set equality over the
entire input range. We then establish a cost dichotomy: in a flat operation model
the relative saving is $(s-c)/s - A/(sk)$, strictly increasing in the pool size
$k$ with ceiling $(s-c)/s$, and there is no crossover whenever $A < s-c$; in a
word-operation model a balanced product tree over $2^L$ leaves of $w$ words costs
$w^2(4^L-2^L)/2$ word operations, quadratic in the pool size, so the sign reverses,
with a unique crossover $M^{*} = 1 + (s_1-c_1)/q$ calibrated to the measured
$M^{*}\approx 1715$ candidates. Both regimes are the single per-candidate cost
$A/k + c + q(k-1)$, strictly decreasing when $q=0$ and minimised uniquely at
$k^{*}=\sqrt{A/q}$ with value $c-q+2\sqrt{Aq}$ when $q>0$. Finally, with testing
occupying a fraction $f = 11.56\%$ of per-factor work, no testing improvement can
save more than $f$ overall and the end-to-end speedup factor is at most $1/(1-f)$;
inverting the measurement, an overall gain of $0.104$ forces the residual testing
cost to be exactly $29/289 \approx 10.03\%$. A relation quota completes the
picture: more than $\pi(B)$ positive $B$-smooth relations always contain a
nonempty sub-family with square product, so $26$ relations suffice at $B=100$.
\end{abstract}

