\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The setting}
Let $B \ge 2$. A positive integer $n$ is \emph{$B$-smooth} if every prime $p$
dividing $n$ satisfies $p \le B$. Smooth numbers are the currency of
subexponential factoring: the quadratic sieve and the number field sieve generate
a stream of candidates, retain the $B$-smooth ones as \emph{relations}, and solve
a linear system over $\FF_2$ on their exponent vectors to produce a congruence of
squares.

Deciding smoothness is executed on every candidate and is therefore a first-order
cost. The naive method is \emph{solo trial division}: divide each candidate by
each prime of the factor base, at cost proportional to $\pi(B)$ per candidate,
with nothing shared between candidates. Bernstein's \emph{batch smoothness test}
removes that isolation: it forms one large integer $P_B$, the product of the
factor base, and processes an entire pool against $P_B$ in a single remainder-tree
cascade, so that the cost shape becomes a once-paid setup plus a small
per-candidate term.

\subsection{What is claimed and what is true}
A controlled experiment compared the two arms at $B = 100$, candidate bit length
$40$, pool sizes $k \in \{1,8,64,512\}$, with the finding phase identical across
arms by construction. Three claims emerged: (i) the batch-detected smooth set
matched trial division on $500/500$ samples in each of three variants;
(ii) in a flat operation model batch beat solo at every pool size, best relative
gain $+0.104$ at $k=512$, while a word-operation model reversed the sign at large
pools with crossover $M^{*}\approx 1715$; (iii) solo testing is only $11.56\%$ of
per-factor work.

This paper audits all three. Claim (i) is weaker than the truth and is strengthened
to a theorem over the whole input range. Claim (ii) is a statement about
calibration constants rather than about batching, and we isolate the inequality
responsible. Claim (iii) is correct and can be inverted into a measuring
instrument. As a by-product we make precise what the relations are for, and why
no factorizations were obtained.

\subsection{Honest placement}
Batch smoothness testing is standard machinery, and nothing here moves an
asymptotic boundary. What is offered is a proof, rather than a sample, of
exactness on the full input range including the shortcuts implementations take;
exact closed forms for both cost regimes and their unification; and a
theorem-level Amdahl bound that converts two aggregate percentages into a
phase-level speedup.

\section{The batch modulus and the exactness criterion}

\subsection{The modulus}

\begin{definition}[Factor-base product]
For $B \in \NN$ set
\[
P_B \;=\; \prod_{\substack{p \le B\\ p\ \text{prime}}} p,
\]
the product of the primes at most $B$: the value held at the root of the product
tree over the factor base.
\end{definition}

Being a product of positive integers, $P_B > 0$; in particular $P_B \neq 0$, which
every factorization argument below requires.

\begin{lemma}[Squarefreeness]\label{lem:sqfree}
If $p$ is prime and $p \le B$, then the exponent of $p$ in $P_B$ equals $1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The factorization of a product is the sum of the factorizations. Each factor of
$P_B$ is a prime $q \le B$ whose factorization is the indicator of $q$; summing
over the factor base, the coefficient of $p$ receives the single term $q = p$
(present since $p$ is prime and $p \le B$) and $0$ from every other term.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Membership criterion]\label{lem:member}
For $p$ prime, $p \mid P_B$ if and only if $p \le B$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $p \le B$ then by Lemma~\ref{lem:sqfree} the exponent of $p$ in $P_B$ is
positive, so $p \mid P_B$. Conversely a prime dividing a finite product divides
some factor, which is a prime $q \le B$; $p \mid q$ with both prime forces
$p = q \le B$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:member} says the batch modulus \emph{is} the factor base: it encodes
membership as divisibility, which is exactly what lets one integer stand in for
$\pi(B)$ separate divisions.

\subsection{Smoothness}

\begin{definition}[Smoothness]
$\Smooth_B(n)$ holds if for every prime $p$ with $p \mid n$ one has $p \le B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Decidable form]\label{lem:dec}
For $n \neq 0$, $\Smooth_B(n)$ holds if and only if every element of the finite set
of prime factors of $n$ is at most $B$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Membership in the prime-factor set of a nonzero $n$ is precisely the conjunction
``prime and divides''; both directions are unfolding.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:dec} is what solo trial division decides. The next theorem shows a
completely different computation decides the same predicate.

\subsection{The criterion}

\begin{theorem}[Batch Smoothness Criterion --- exactness]\label{thm:crit}
Let $B, t \in \NN$ and $0 < n < 2^t$. Then
\[
\Smooth_B(n) \iff n \mid P_B^{\,t}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\emph{Soundness $(\Leftarrow)$.} If $p$ is prime with $p \mid n$ then
$p \mid P_B^{\,t}$; a prime dividing a power divides the base, so $p \mid P_B$ and
$p \le B$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:member}. Neither positivity nor the size bound is used.

\emph{Completeness $(\Rightarrow)$.} Since $n>0$ and $P_B^{\,t}\neq 0$,
divisibility is equivalent to a pointwise inequality of factorizations, so it
suffices to bound $v_p(n) \le v_p(P_B^{\,t})$ for every prime $p$. If $p \nmid n$
then $v_p(n)=0$. Otherwise $p \le B$ by smoothness, so
$v_p(P_B^{\,t}) = t\cdot 1 = t$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:sqfree}. Writing $e = v_p(n)$ we
have $p^{e} \mid n$, hence
\[
2^{e} \le p^{e} \le n < 2^{t},
\]
using $p \ge 2$ and $n>0$. Strict monotonicity of $x \mapsto 2^{x}$ gives $e < t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The two directions differ in content. Soundness is a statement about primes and is
essentially free; completeness is quantitative and is the only place the
bit-length hypothesis enters, converting size information into exponent
information via the minimality of $2$ among primes.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Sharpness}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the exponent]\label{thm:sharp}
For $B \ge 2$ and all $t, s \in \NN$,
\[
2^{t} \mid P_B^{\,s} \iff t \le s.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:sqfree} with $p = 2$ (legitimate as $2 \le B$), the exponent of
$2$ in $P_B$ is $1$, hence in $P_B^{\,s}$ it is $s$. For a prime power,
$p^{t}\mid m$ with $m \neq 0$ iff $t \le v_p(m)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The size bound is indispensable]
For every $B \ge 2$, the number $4$ is $B$-smooth yet $4 \nmid P_B^{\,1}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Any prime dividing $4 = 2^2$ equals $2 \le B$, so $4$ is smooth. If
$2^2 \mid P_B^{\,1}$ then Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} forces $2 \le 1$.
\end{proof}

Hence $t$ cannot be replaced by any smaller function of the candidate: the family
$n = 2^t$ saturates the bound for every $t$. In practice the exponent must track
the \emph{bit length} of the candidates, not their number nor the factor base size.

\subsection{The shortcuts implementations take}

\begin{theorem}[Repeated-squaring form]\label{thm:sq}
If $0 < n < 2^t$ and $t \le 2^{e}$, then $\Smooth_B(n) \iff n \mid P_B^{\,2^{e}}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $t \le 2^{e}$ we get $2^{t} \le 2^{2^{e}}$, so $n < 2^{2^{e}}$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:crit} with $2^{e}$ in place of $t$.
\end{proof}

Thus $e$ squarings suffice as soon as $2^{e}$ dominates the bit length: for
$t = 40$, $e = 6$.

\begin{theorem}[Remainder-tree form]\label{thm:mod}
If $0 < n < 2^t$ then
\[
\Smooth_B(n) \iff \bigl(P_B \bmod n\bigr)^{t} \bmod n = 0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:crit} with $(a \bmod n)^{t}\equiv a^{t} \pmod n$ and the
equivalence of divisibility with vanishing remainder.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:mod} licenses the algorithm: the huge integer $P_B$ enters each
candidate only through the residue $P_B \bmod n < n$, after which all operands have
the candidate's own size. The remainder tree computes all $k$ residues in one
cascade, and the $e$ squarings are few-word operations.

\subsection{Tree shape is irrelevant}

\begin{definition}[Product tree]
A \emph{product tree} is a finite binary tree with naturals at the leaves, with
value $\val(\mathrm{leaf}(n)) = n$, $\val(\mathrm{node}(L,R)) = \val(L)\val(R)$,
and leaf list $\lv(\mathrm{leaf}(n)) = [n]$,
$\lv(\mathrm{node}(L,R)) = \lv(L) \mathbin{+\!\!+} \lv(R)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Shape independence]\label{thm:shape}
For every product tree $T$, $\val(T)$ equals the product of the entries of
$\lv(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Structural induction: leaves are immediate, and at a node the value is the product
of the subtree values, which by induction are the products of the two leaf
sublists; the product of a concatenation is the product of the products.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $\lv(T)$ is any permutation of the factor base $\{p : p \le B\}$, then
$\val(T) = P_B$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:shape} the value is the product over the leaf list, and a list
product is permutation invariant, giving the product over the factor base.
\end{proof}

This is why the ``tree'' and ``direct'' arms of the audit could not disagree: they
compute the same modulus and hence test the same divisibility. Any disagreement
would indicate an implementation bug, never a difference of method.

\subsection{The audit, as a theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact-match audit]\label{thm:audit}
Let $B, t \in \NN$ and let $S$ be a finite set of candidates with $0 < n < 2^{t}$
for all $n \in S$. Then
\[
\{\, n \in S : n \mid P_B^{\,t} \,\}
\;=\;
\{\, n \in S : \text{every prime factor of } n \text{ is } \le B \,\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two filters agree when their predicates agree pointwise; for $n \in S$ we have
$0 < n < 2^{t}$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:dec} and Theorem~\ref{thm:crit} apply.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The measured audit instance]
With $B = 100$, $t = 9$ and $S = \{1,\dots,500\}$ (every candidate below
$2^{9} = 512$), the batch-detected and trial-detected smooth sets coincide.
\end{corollary}

The methodological point: ``$500/500$ samples agreed'' is a statement about $500$
integers, while there are on the order of $10^{12}$ candidates of bit length $40$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:audit} makes agreement a property of the predicates rather than of
the sample.

\section{The cost dichotomy}

\subsection{Product-tree operation counts}

\begin{definition}[Flat op count]
Let $\FlatOps(0) = 0$ and $\FlatOps(L+1) = 2\FlatOps(L) + 1$: the number of
multiplications in a balanced product tree over $2^{L}$ leaves, one per internal
node.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:flatops}
$\FlatOps(L) + 1 = 2^{L}$, i.e.\ the tree has $2^{L}-1$ internal nodes.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: $0 + 1 = 1$, and $2(2^{L}-1)+1+1 = 2^{L+1}$.
\end{proof}

In the flat model the tree is therefore \emph{linear} in the pool size.

\begin{definition}[Word cost]
With schoolbook multiplication, multiplying two $m$-word integers costs $m^{2}$
word operations. Set $\WordCost(w,0) = 0$ and
\[
\WordCost(w, L+1) \;=\; 2\WordCost(w,L) + \bigl(2^{L}w\bigr)^{2},
\]
the last term being the topmost multiplication of two operands of $2^{L}w$ words.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:wordclosed}
For all $w, L$,
\[
2\WordCost(w,L) + w^{2}2^{L} \;=\; w^{2}4^{L},
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
\WordCost(w,L) = \frac{w^{2}\bigl(4^{L} - 2^{L}\bigr)}{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $L$; base case $0 + w^{2} = w^{2}$. For the step use
$4^{L+1} = 4\cdot 4^{L}$, $2^{L+1} = 2\cdot 2^{L}$ and
$(2^{L}w)^{2} = w^{2}4^{L}$ (from $4^{L} = 2^{L}2^{L}$), then substitute the
inductive identity. The additive phrasing avoids truncated subtraction over $\NN$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$w^{2}\,2^{L}\bigl(2^{L}-1\bigr) \le 2\WordCost(w,L)$.
\end{corollary}

The essential content is that \emph{the same tree is linear in the flat model and
quadratic in the word model}; every later disagreement descends from this.

\subsection{The flat model: amortization without crossover}

Let $A$ be the one-off setup cost, $c$ the per-candidate batch cost, $s$ the
per-candidate solo cost.

\begin{definition}
$\Batch(k) = A + ck$, $\Solo(k) = sk$, and the relative saving is
$\sigma(k) = 1 - \Batch(k)/\Solo(k)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit saving]\label{thm:flatsaving}
For $s > 0$ and $k > 0$,
\[
\sigma(k) \;=\; \frac{s-c}{s} \;-\; \frac{A}{sk}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$1 - (A+ck)/(sk) = (sk - ck - A)/(sk)$.
\end{proof}

A \emph{ceiling} $(s-c)/s$, attained with free setup, minus an \emph{amortized
setup term} $A/(sk)$ decaying like $1/k$.

\begin{theorem}[No crossover]\label{thm:nocross}
If $0 \le A < s-c$ then $\Batch(k) < \Solo(k)$ for every $k \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Solo(k)-\Batch(k) = (s-c)k - A = (s-c) - A + (s-c)(k-1) > 0$.
\end{proof}

Thus ``batch beats solo at every pool size'' is \emph{equivalent} to a calibration
inequality among three implementation constants, not a property of batching.

\begin{theorem}[Monotone amortization]
If $s>0$, $A>0$ and $0 < k_1 < k_2$, then $\sigma(k_1) < \sigma(k_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:flatsaving} the only $k$-dependence is $-A/(sk)$, and
$A/(sk_2) < A/(sk_1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict ceiling and limit]
For $s>0$, $A>0$, $k>0$ one has $\sigma(k) < (s-c)/s$, and
$\sigma(k) \to (s-c)/s$ as $k \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The gap is exactly $A/(sk) > 0$, which tends to $0$.
\end{proof}

Amortization can eliminate the setup cost entirely and can never touch the
per-candidate cost; the measured $+0.104$ at $k = 512$ therefore sits below a hard
ceiling $(s-c)/s$ that no pool size can breach.

\subsection{The word model: the sign reverses}

\begin{theorem}[Word-model reversal]
If $2s + w^{2} < w^{2}2^{L}$, then $s\,2^{L} < \WordCost(w,L)$: the factor-base
tree alone costs more word operations than all of solo trial division on the same
pool.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiply the hypothesis by $2^{L} > 0$ to get $2s2^{L} + w^{2}2^{L} < w^{2}4^{L}$,
and compare with $w^{2}4^{L} = 2\WordCost(w,L) + w^{2}2^{L}$ from
Theorem~\ref{thm:wordclosed}; cancel $w^{2}2^{L}$.
\end{proof}

The threshold depends only on $s/w^{2}$, so the reversal is unavoidable for
schoolbook arithmetic. Concretely at $w = 8$ words against $s = 1000$ word
operations per candidate, pools of $2^{6} = 64$ already lose.

\begin{definition}[Continuous word model]
$\BatchW(k) = qk(k-1) + c_1 k$ and $\SoloW(k) = s_1 k$, with $q > 0$ the quadratic
big-integer coefficient and $c_1$ the linear per-candidate term.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Unique crossover, closed form]\label{thm:cross}
For $q > 0$ and $k > 0$,
\[
\BatchW(k) \le \SoloW(k) \iff k \le 1 + \frac{s_1 - c_1}{q}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Divide by $k>0$: the inequality is $q(k-1)+c_1 \le s_1$, i.e.\
$qk \le q + s_1 - c_1$, i.e.\ $k \le 1 + (s_1-c_1)/q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Calibration]
The reported crossover $M^{*}\approx 1715$ is exactly $s_1 - c_1 = 1714q$; under it
$\BatchW(k) \le \SoloW(k)$ iff $k \le 1715$. In particular batch is favourable at
$k = 512$ and unfavourable at $k = 4096$: the sign changes inside the deployment
range.
\end{corollary}

Whether batching helps thus depends on which operations are charged, and the answer
flips inside the range of pool sizes one would actually use.

\subsection{Unification: one formula, both regimes}

\begin{definition}[Block cost]
For a stream processed in blocks of size $k$,
\[
\cost(k) \;=\; \frac{A}{k} + c + q(k-1),
\]
with $A$ the per-block setup, $c$ the flat per-candidate cost, $q \ge 0$ the
quadratic penalty.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Degenerate case $q = 0$]
If $A > 0$ and $0 < k_1 < k_2$ then $\cost(k_2) < \cost(k_1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $q = 0$ the cost is $A/k + c$, strictly decreasing for $A>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[AM--GM lower bound]
If $A, q > 0$ and $k > 0$ then $\cost(k) \ge c - q + 2\sqrt{Aq}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(\sqrt{A/k}-\sqrt{qk})^{2}\ge 0$ gives $A/k + qk \ge 2\sqrt{Aq}$; add $c-q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment and uniqueness]\label{thm:opt}
For $A, q > 0$ and $k > 0$,
\[
\cost(k) = c - q + 2\sqrt{Aq} \iff k = k^{*} := \sqrt{A/q}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At $k = \sqrt{A/q}$ one has $A/k = qk = \sqrt{Aq}$, giving the value. Conversely
equality in AM--GM forces $(\sqrt{A/k}-\sqrt{qk})^{2}=0$, hence $A/k = qk$, hence
$k^{2} = A/q$ and $k = \sqrt{A/q}$ as $k > 0$.
\end{proof}

The two ``phenomena'' are the branches $q = 0$ and $q > 0$ of one curve, and the
optimal batch size is a square root of a cost ratio, not a function of tree depth
or factor base. With $A = 1000$ and $q = 1/1000$ one finds $k^{*} = 1000$
candidates, the same order of magnitude as the measured $M^{*}\approx 1715$.

\section{The Amdahl ceiling}

Let per-factor work split into a finding phase of cost $F \ge 0$ and a testing
phase of cost $S > 0$, with testing share $f = S/(F+S)$; the measured value is
$f = 0.1156$.

\begin{theorem}[Amdahl cap]\label{thm:cap}
For $F \ge 0$, $S > 0$ and any replacement testing cost $S' \ge 0$,
\[
\frac{(F+S)-(F+S')}{F+S} \;\le\; \frac{S}{F+S} \;=\; f .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The numerator is $S - S' \le S$ since $S' \ge 0$, and $F+S > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bounded speedup factor]
For $F, S > 0$ and $S' \ge 0$,
\[
\frac{F+S}{F+S'} \;\le\; \frac{F+S}{F} \;=\; \frac{1}{1-f}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$F+S' \ge F > 0$ and $x \mapsto (F+S)/x$ is decreasing; $1-f = F/(F+S)$.
\end{proof}

At $f = 0.1156$ the ceiling is $1/(1-f)\approx 1.131$: a constant factor, so no
testing improvement can change the asymptotic class. The measured
$+0.104 < 0.1156$ lies below the cap by necessity, and even an ideal arm could not
reach $+0.12$.

\begin{theorem}[Phase residual]\label{thm:residual}
With $S > 0$, $F+S > 0$ and overall saving $d = \bigl((F+S)-(F+S')\bigr)/(F+S)$,
\[
\frac{S'}{S} \;=\; 1 - d\cdot\frac{F+S}{S} \;=\; 1 - \frac{d}{f}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The definition of $d$ gives $S - S' = d(F+S)$, so $S' = S - d(F+S)$; divide by $S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Inverting the measurement]
With $f = 1156/10000$ and $d = 104/1000$,
\[
\frac{S'}{S} \;=\; 1 - \frac{0.104}{0.1156} \;=\; \frac{29}{289} \;\approx\;
10.03\%,
\]
a phase-level speedup of $\approx 9.97\times$.
\end{corollary}

Two aggregate numbers therefore determine the phase-level speedup exactly, with no
instrumentation of the phase. The headline is not ``a $10\%$ faster
factorization'' but ``a $10\times$ faster testing phase inside a pipeline where
testing is one part in nine''.

\section{The relation quota}

\begin{theorem}[Subset pigeonhole over $\FF_2$]\label{thm:pigeon}
Let $\iota, \kappa$ be finite index sets with $|\kappa| < |\iota|$ and let
$v : \iota \to \FF_2^{\kappa}$ be any family. Then there is a nonempty
$S \subseteq \iota$ with $\sum_{i \in S} v_i = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
There are $2^{|\iota|}$ subsets but only $2^{|\kappa|} < 2^{|\iota|}$ possible
subset sums, so two distinct subsets $S \neq T$ have equal sums. Put
$U = (S\setminus T)\cup(T\setminus S)$, nonempty since $S \neq T$. Splitting $S$
and $T$ into intersection and difference and cancelling the common intersection
sum gives $\sum_{S\setminus T} v = \sum_{T\setminus S} v$; adding these equal sums
over the disjoint union $U$ yields $0$ in characteristic $2$.
\end{proof}

No injectivity is assumed: repeated relations are permitted, which matters because
a real batch produces duplicates.

\begin{theorem}[Even exponents give squares]\label{thm:even}
If $n \neq 0$ and $v_p(n)$ is even for every prime $p$, then $n$ is a perfect
square.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $m = \prod_p p^{v_p(n)/2}$ over the finite support; then
$m\cdot m = \prod_p p^{v_p(n)} = n$ by evenness and factorization reconstruction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Relation quota]\label{thm:quota}
Let $n_1,\dots,n_m$ be positive $B$-smooth integers with $m > \pi(B)$. Then some
nonempty $S \subseteq \{1,\dots,m\}$ has $\prod_{i\in S} n_i$ a perfect square.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Index coordinates by the factor base and send each relation to its exponent vector
mod $2$, $v_i(p) = v_p(n_i) \bmod 2$. As $m > \pi(B)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon}
supplies a nonempty $S$ with $\sum_{i\in S} v_i = 0$. Put $N = \prod_{i\in S} n_i
\neq 0$. For $p \le B$ prime, $v_p(N) = \sum_{i \in S} v_p(n_i)$ is even by the
vanishing coordinate; for $p > B$ prime each term vanishes by smoothness; for $p$
non-prime each term vanishes. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:even}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\pi(100) = 25$, so any $26$ positive $100$-smooth relations contain a nonempty
sub-family with square product.
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:quota} guarantees that the linear-algebra stage cannot fail once
the quota is met; conversely no testing speed produces a split before it is met.
The experiment's zero split count at bit length $40$ and $B = 100$ is thus a yield
deficit: the pipeline never accumulated $26$ relations, the smooth density at those
parameters being of order $10^{-4}$.

\begin{remark}[A caveat on yield]
An elementary upper bound on the count $\Psi(x,B)$ of $B$-smooth numbers below $x$
by counting admissible exponent vectors gives, at $x = 2^{40}$ and $B = 100$,
\[
\Psi(2^{40},100) \;\le\; \prod_{p \le 100}\left(1 + \frac{40}{\log_2 p}\right)
\;\approx\; 10^{22},
\]
which exceeds $2^{40}\approx 10^{12}$ and is therefore vacuous. A nontrivial yield
statement requires Dickman-type input; the observed density $\approx 10^{-4}$ is
empirical and is not claimed as proved.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection*{Factor-base product tree}
Place the primes $p_1 < \cdots < p_r \le B$ at the leaves and repeatedly replace
consecutive pairs by their product until one value remains. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:shape} the result is $P_B$ regardless of pairing order. Flat cost:
$r-1$ multiplications (Proposition~\ref{prop:flatops}). Word cost with schoolbook
multiplication and $w$-word leaves: $w^{2}(4^{L}-2^{L})/2$ for $r = 2^{L}$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:wordclosed}). Executed once per run.

\subsection*{Remainder tree}
Build the product tree of the candidates $n_1,\dots,n_k$, store
$P_B \bmod \val(\text{root})$ at the root, and at each child store the parent's
value reduced modulo the child's value. The leaves then hold $P_B \bmod n_i$. The
invariant --- each stored value is congruent to $P_B$ modulo the subtree product ---
is preserved because a child's value divides its parent's.

\subsection*{Batch smoothness filter}
Choose $e$ with $2^{e} \ge t$ (for $t = 40$, $e = 6$). For each leaf residue
$r_i = P_B \bmod n_i$, square $e$ times modulo $n_i$ and report $n_i$ smooth
exactly when the result is $0$. Correctness is Theorems~\ref{thm:sq}
and~\ref{thm:mod}; exactness against trial division on the whole pool is
Theorem~\ref{thm:audit}.

\subsection*{Cost-model selection}
Given $A, c, s, q$: if $q = 0$ choose the largest feasible pool; if $q > 0$ choose
$k^{*} = \sqrt{A/q}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:opt}) and check the crossover
$M^{*} = 1 + (s-c)/q$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:cross}). Report the achievable overall gain
as the phase gain scaled by $f$ and capped by $f$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}).

\section{Discussion}

\emph{The exactness result is a theorem and stays one.} The criterion decides
$B$-smoothness exactly for all $0 < n < 2^{t}$; the exponent is minimal; the
modular and squaring shortcuts are exact; tree shape is immaterial; and the batch
filter equals the trial-division filter on every pool. None of this depends on
hardware, model, or calibration, and the saturating family $n = 2^{t}$ shows the
bit-length hypothesis cannot be relaxed.

\emph{The performance result is a calibration.} ``Batch wins at every pool size''
is equivalent to $A < s-c$; word-level accounting reverses the conclusion past
$M^{*}\approx 1715$. The correct summary is that the per-candidate cost is
$A/k + c + q(k-1)$ and everything follows from whether $q$ vanishes --- a statement
about the multiplication routine, not the algorithm.

\emph{The significance was capped before the experiment ran.} With testing at
$11.56\%$ of per-factor work the ceiling is $11.56\%$ overall and $1.131\times$
end-to-end: constant-shaving on a known method, zero class movement. The
consolation is the inversion, which shows the experiment did measure a
$\approx 9.97\times$ testing speedup that simply could not translate into more than
$10.4\%$ overall.

The relation quota indicates where to look next: the bottleneck was smooth
\emph{yield}, not testing speed. Twenty-six relations suffice at $B = 100$, and the
run never obtained them.

\section{Future work}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Sub-quadratic multiplication.} With multiplication exponent
$\mu \in (1,2]$ the block cost should become $A/k + c + qk^{\mu-1}$, giving
$k^{*} \propto (A/q)^{1/\mu}$; Theorem~\ref{thm:opt} is the case $\mu = 2$.
Karatsuba ($\mu = \log_2 3$) and FFT-based multiplication ($\mu \to 1$) give direct
predictions, the latter predicting the disappearance of the interior optimum.
\item \textbf{Provable yield.} The elementary bound on $\Psi(x,B)$ is vacuous at
the relevant parameters; a usable statement needs Dickman-type input and would turn
the observed $10^{-4}$ density into a theorem.
\item \textbf{Calibration as a first-class object.} Since ``batch wins'' reduces to
$A < s-c$, systematic measurement of $(A,c,s,q)$ across architectures and bignum
libraries would replace anecdote with a phase diagram in the $(k,\mu)$ plane.
\item \textbf{Beyond the testing phase.} The Amdahl analysis applies verbatim to the
finding phase, which holds the remaining $88.44\%$ and is where any end-to-end
improvement must come from.
\end{itemize}

